3. Title: Self-Authored Items — First Read Tried the ⟦TERM⟧ approach this cycle since our ⟦X⟧ keeps flagging holes faster than we can hand-author replacements.
4. The ⟦X⟧ specifically called out the mid-difficulty tier, so that's where we pointed the ⟦TERM⟧ experiment first. Had the frontier agent draft two hundred candidates and ran a second model as filter to strip anything it judged trivially easy for the author.
5. Had the frontier agent draft two hundred candidates and ran a second model as filter to strip anything it judged trivially easy for the author. ⟦TERM⟧ are a decent stopgap for volume but I wouldn't trust them yet as the sole source for a capability claim — the filter missed easy-for-author items more than once, and the batch still skewed soft on the task family the author agent is best at.
6. Mixed in a small set of ⟦TERM⟧ too, generated by last quarter's checkpoint instead of the current one, specifically to dodge the difficulty-skew problem you get when the author and the evaluated agent are the same model. The ⟦TERM⟧ held up better than I expected once we checked them against the control set — no obvious softness toward the older checkpoint's habits.
DEFINITION:
⟦TERM⟧ is a procedure for producing test items (such as evaluation questions or candidate bugs) at a wanted difficulty tier when hand-authoring them can't keep up with demand — for example, when a checking process flags gaps faster than people can write replacements. One model (an agent or checkpoint) drafts a batch of candidate items; a second, separate model then filters that batch, removing any item it judges trivially easy for the author. The output is a pool of items, each labeled either "trivial for the author" (dropped) or kept as valid. The filter must not share the author's blind spots, or the kept set stays soft on tasks the author is good at; using a different checkpoint as author than the agent being evaluated reduces this overlap. The items are only a stopgap for volume, not a trustworthy sole basis for a capability claim, and should be checked against a control set.

ITEM 5
EXCERPTS:
1. Set a ⟦TERM⟧ of three on the new codebase-navigation benchmark, figuring it'd force the agent to actually hold file contents in working memory instead of treating disk as free scratch space. First real run, agent fails outright — opened auth.py four times chasing down a decorator it kept forgetting the signature of.
