2. We kept a suite alive for a year that stopped telling us anything for probably the last four months of it. Tried to console myself by running ⟦TERM⟧ on the surviving 39%, pairing each item with a reworded twin — same ask, different formatting, bullet list versus prose. Gap was smaller than I expected, only 4 points on average, which is actually reassuring since it means the items still doing work aren't just measuring who parses markdown better.
3. Ran ⟦TERM⟧ on the survivors before publishing, same task with plain-text phrasing against the version with our usual boilerplate formatting. The ⟦TERM⟧ gap came out around six points on the ouroboros batch versus two points on the hand-authored control, which reads as presentation cost, not capability, and it's bigger here than expected.
4. Ran ⟦TERM⟧ on the survivors before publishing, same task with plain-text phrasing against the version with our usual boilerplate formatting. The ⟦TERM⟧ gap came out around six points on the ouroboros batch versus two points on the hand-authored control, which reads as presentation cost, not capability, and it's bigger here than expected.
5. Before merging any of it we ran ⟦TERM⟧ across the new items, wording-only variants of the same twenty tasks.
6. Before merging any of it we ran ⟦TERM⟧ across the new items, wording-only variants of the same twenty tasks. The ⟦TERM⟧ spread was tighter on the ⟦X⟧ batch than on our hand-authored baseline, which is a decent sign the phrasing isn't doing the discriminating work instead of the task content.
DEFINITION:
⟦TERM⟧ is a robustness check run on paired test items before they are trusted (e.g., before publishing or merging). For each item you take one underlying task and produce two or more variants that reword or reformat it while keeping the actual ask constant — casual versus formal phrasing, plain text versus boilerplate formatting, bullet list versus prose. You score each variant and compute the gap, the average point difference between the paired variants' scores. A small gap (e.g., 4 points) means the items measure task capability; a large gap means presentation cost is inflating or depressing scores rather than skill. You compare the gap against a baseline control set (such as hand-authored items) to judge whether it is larger or smaller than expected (e.g., six points versus two).

